<h1>AI FOMO is real</h1>

<div class="post">
<p>Now that I'm a Product Director in collegiate sports compliance software (a mouthful, I know), I see the landscape changing, and I worry that I won't be able to keep up.</p>
<p>When I was a Senior PM, the use cases were obvious: creating a custom GPT to help synthesize research, write PRDs, plan roadmaps, and draft project status updates. AI served as an execution partner. Easy. Straightforward, even.</p>
<p>Now I'm less in the weeds, and there's this quiet, nagging voice that says: What if I fall behind? What if the transformation happens around me while I'm in stakeholder meetings?</p>

<h2>The use case shift</h2>
<p>I still use AI for execution (shoutout to working at a startup where title doesn't matter as much as getting the job done). But increasingly, I'm finding the real leverage is as a thought partner, not a task executor.</p>

<h2>What that looks like in practice</h2>
<ul>
<li>Pressure-testing a strategy before I bring it to stakeholders</li>
<li>Spotting patterns across data and products faster</li>
<li>Asking "what am I missing?" before a big decision (and actually getting a useful answer)</li>
<li>Staying current across domains I don't have time to go deep on</li>
</ul>

<p class="pull">The shift is from doing things faster to thinking better.</p>
<p>I don't have this fully figured out. I'm genuinely still building the muscle and knowledge. But I think the leaders who crack this might have more leverage than ever.</p>
</div>

<div class="close">
